INT. TOLL BOOTH UNDER THE TROLL BRIDGE - DAWN

A stone hut slung beneath a humped bridge. Moss. A river chuckles below. A hand-lettered sign: PAY OR PASS (DON'T PASS).

BRAMBLE, 100, a troll in a postman's cap, sits behind a counter sorting copper coins. Her skin is the color of a wet slate, her fingers like roots. A herd of goats shuffles at her feet.

BRAMBLE
(to goats)
Morning, you lot. Don't touch the ledger.

A shadow falls across the booth window. ISKANDAR, 30s, a merchant in a long green coat, stands with a donkey cart. He bows.

ISKANDAR
Good morning, tollkeeper.

BRAMBLE
Toll's two coppers.

ISKANDAR
That's very reasonable.

BRAMBLE
It's the rate.

ISKANDAR
Here is four, to be generous.

Bramble takes the coins. She bites one. She looks at him with narrowed, amber eyes.

BRAMBLE
(muttering)
Nobody pays four.

ISKANDAR
What was that?

BRAMBLE
Nothing. Weather. Cross.

He nods, relieved, and starts the cart. She lets him get three steps before she raises her voice.

BRAMBLE (CONT'D)
(to the goats, loud)
Everyone, we have a customer who pays double!

The goats lift their heads as one.

ISKANDAR
(stopping)
Is something the matter?

BRAMBLE
No, no. Only that the last man who paid double was a king in disguise.

ISKANDAR
I'm not a king.

BRAMBLE
Of course not. Kings never are.

She comes out from behind the counter. She is much taller than the booth suggested. The goats back away from her.

ISKANDAR
I think I'll pay for the road in kind.

BRAMBLE
(to him, soft)
You'll pay the first thing you meet on the far side.

ISKANDAR
That's an old story.

BRAMBLE
It's an old toll.

From the far end of the bridge, a bell rings. A small figure in a red coat is running toward the cart, arms wide, calling.

ISKANDAR
(a whisper)
Leila.

Bramble watches him. Her face does not change.

BRAMBLE
(to herself)
Always the child.
